## 4. Implement genuine persistence and backend functionality

The application currently has no verified working backend. Correct this without unnecessarily changing the existing architecture.

### Inspect and reuse existing infrastructure

If the project already has a configured backend or database, complete and repair that integration.

If it does not, select a suitable persistent backend compatible with the current framework. Firebase Authentication with Cloud Firestore is one possible option, but use an existing supported provider when it makes more sense.

Do not introduce multiple competing backends.

### Required persistent data

The application must persist:

- Income transactions.
- Expense transactions.
- Monthly spending budget.
- Relevant user preferences.
- Theme preference, where appropriate.

Each transaction should have a stable ID, authenticated user ID, transaction type, amount, category, description, transaction date, optional note, and appropriate creation/update timestamps.

Use the project's existing schema conventions if they already satisfy these requirements.

### Data ownership and security

Every user's financial records must be associated with their authenticated user ID.

Enforce access control through the backend and database security rules, not just frontend filtering.

Users must not be able to read, modify, or delete another user's records by changing a request or transaction ID.

Never expose private API keys, privileged database credentials, or server secrets in browser code.

### Authentication integration

Inspect the existing Google sign-in and email registration/login flows.

Connect them to genuine authentication if they are currently only visual interfaces.

Preserve working authentication integrations.

A new user must receive a clean account. A returning user must retrieve their own previously saved data.

Do not display financial records before authentication and authorization have been established.

### Transaction synchronization

After a successful add, edit, or delete operation:

- Persist the change.
- Update the dashboard.
- Update recent transactions.
- Update history.
- Recalculate all affected financial metrics.
- Refresh the relevant charts.
- Refresh or invalidate AI insight data as appropriate.

Changes must remain correct after refreshing the page, signing out and back in, or reopening the application.

Provide loading, success, and error states. Never silently pretend a database write succeeded when it failed.

If external credentials, provider setup, OAuth configuration, or database rules are required, implement what can safely be implemented and report the exact outstanding configuration. Do not fabricate a working connection.

