## 9. Replace confusing financial terminology in Trends

The Trends page is structurally acceptable. Preserve its existing charts and layout wherever they work correctly.

Make targeted changes to improve the terminology and calculations.

### Replace "Net Surplus"

The term "Net Surplus" is unnecessarily technical for an everyday expense tracker.

Replace it with:

**"Money Left"**

Calculate:

Money Left = Total Income − Total Expenses

Make the reporting period explicit so users understand what the figure represents.

If the period's income is ₹25,000 and expenses are ₹13,000, Money Left should be ₹12,000.

Do not confuse this metric with the user's complete historical account balance or with the remaining monthly spending budget.

Use a short explanatory subtitle if needed.

### Replace "Savings Rate"

Remove the existing Savings Rate metric from the MVP.

Replace it with:

**"Monthly Budget Used"**

This should show the percentage of the monthly spending budget consumed by recorded expenses.

For example:

Monthly budget = ₹30,000

Current-month expenses = ₹3,000

Monthly Budget Used = 10%

Use the same underlying calculation and current-month expense dataset as the dashboard budget component.

If the user has not set a budget, display a clear "Set a budget to track spending" message or equivalent action instead of inventing a percentage.

### Keep the rest of Trends functional

Preserve the existing income-versus-expense chart and expense category breakdown where appropriate.

Verify that they use actual transactions and update after data changes.

Retain useful date-range selection and category information if already implemented.

Ensure all chart labels, tooltips, legends, and summary values are understandable to a nontechnical user.

Do not invent trends for users with insufficient history.

